+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Running a guest with SSH forwarding
===================================

The package provides a qobsd_<arch> service and login class for each
supported guest architecture. This example installs an HPPA snapshot
in /var/vm/hppa.qcow2.

    # su -c qobsd_hppa -s /bin/sh _vmd ${LOCALBASE}/bin/qobsd \
        -d /var/vm -m -s hppa

After installation, enable and start qobsd_hppa with SSH forwarded
from 127.0.0.1:2201 to the guest.

    # rcctl enable qobsd_hppa
    # rcctl set qobsd_hppa flags -p 2201 hppa
    # rcctl start qobsd_hppa

Connect using an account on the guest.

    $ ssh -p 2201 user@127.0.0.1

On the host, attach to the guest's console with tmux.

    $ doas -u _vmd tmux -N -S /var/vm/qobsd_hppa.sock attach-session

To connect through somehost.home.arpa, add these entries to
~/.ssh/config on the client. The qobsd account in the guest must
authorize the bundled public key.

    Host qobsd-hppa.somehost.home.arpa
      HostKeyAlias qobsd-hppa.somehost.home.arpa
      Port 2201

    Host qobsd-*.somehost.home.arpa
      ProxyJump somehost.home.arpa
      HostName 127.0.0.1
      User qobsd
      IdentityFile /usr/local/share/examples/qobsd/qobsd_ed25519.pem

Connect using the host alias.

    $ ssh qobsd-hppa.somehost.home.arpa

Running three HPPA guests
=========================

Run the installer with -f hppa_2.qcow2 -H qobsd-hppa-2 and then with
-f hppa_3.qcow2 -H qobsd-hppa-3, placing both options before hppa.
Use the same names if the installer asks for a hostname. Each service
needs a link and a matching login class. To add an SSH port, set the
full argument list, including the disk and hostname.

    # ln -s ${RCDIR}/qobsd ${RCDIR}/qobsd_hppa_2
    # ln -s ${RCDIR}/qobsd ${RCDIR}/qobsd_hppa_3
    # sed 's/^qobsd_hppa:/qobsd_hppa_2:/' \
        ${LOCALBASE}/share/examples/login.conf.d/qobsd_hppa \
        > ${SYSCONFDIR}/login.conf.d/qobsd_hppa_2
    # sed 's/^qobsd_hppa:/qobsd_hppa_3:/' \
        ${LOCALBASE}/share/examples/login.conf.d/qobsd_hppa \
        > ${SYSCONFDIR}/login.conf.d/qobsd_hppa_3
    # rcctl enable qobsd_hppa_2 qobsd_hppa_3
    # rcctl set qobsd_hppa_2 flags -f hppa_2.qcow2 \
        -H qobsd-hppa-2 -p 2202 hppa
    # rcctl set qobsd_hppa_3 flags -f hppa_3.qcow2 \
        -H qobsd-hppa-3 -p 2203 hppa
    # rcctl start qobsd_hppa_2 qobsd_hppa_3

Connect to 127.0.0.1 on ports 2201, 2202, and 2203.
